epistle (0.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security: API and JMAP push subscriptions belong to their creator; a
    WebSocket or event-source session closes once its credential is revoked
    or its account disabled; HTTP routes refuse a disabled account's key;
    JMAP reads need the read scope; TLS-RPT reports are posted only to public
    addresses; DMARC reports go only to plain addresses the policy domain
    authorizes; SCRAM verifiers use 600000 iterations and TOTP secrets are
    encrypted at rest; account-bound keys stay within their account.
  * Mail protocols: no second banner inside TLS after STARTTLS (mail
    between two epistle servers failed), commands pipelined behind STARTTLS
    are dropped, DMARC evaluates the From a reader sees, submissions with a
    From the account does not own are refused, DKIM signs for the From
    domain, a rotated DKIM key is used only once DNS shows it, DANE applies
    only behind a DNSSEC-secure MX and never falls back to cleartext on a
    bogus TLSA, every listener survives a failed accept and bounds TLS
    handshakes and HTTP request heads.
  * Sending: the API and JMAP go through the same path as SMTP submission
    (DKIM signed, local recipients delivered directly) and build complete
    messages (text and HTML, attachments, inline parts, threading and list
    headers); Bcc never leaves the server; mail the server's own accounts
    send is scanned for viruses; every stored copy gets Return-Path and
    Delivered-To, and a forwarding loop is bounced; TLS-Required: No is
    honoured for our own senders.
  * MTA-STS: a policy fetch failure is reported in TLS-RPT and a new
    policy id is picked up before max_age.
  * IMAP: new keywords are announced in FLAGS before the FETCH that uses
    them, new messages are announced after APPEND and on NOOP, and a
    LITERAL+ APPEND gets no stray continuation.
  * DNS providers: TXT values reach each of the 13 providers in the form
    its API expects, an update replaces only the record of the same
    purpose, and Namecheap uses the request its API documents (it needs
    `[dns] client_ip` now).
  * DKIM rotation is journalled: a crash at any step completes or rolls
    back on the next start, and `epistle dkim cleanup` removes orphans.
  * JMAP: incremental sync with /changes and /queryChanges from a per-account
    change journal, push over an event source, and the limits the Session
    advertises are enforced.
  * CLI: every resource answers to epistle <resource> <action> with --json
    and consistent exit codes; destructive commands ask first (--yes skips),
    passwords are read without echo, shell completions and man pages ship
    in the package; the account stores lock across CLI and server.
  * Delivery: greylisting persists in PostgreSQL, DMARC reports are signed
    and well formed, ACME supports TLS-ALPN-01 and DNS-01, TLS-RPT reports
    are sent, autoconfig is served on 443, Sieve gains the mailbox
    extension, CalDAV/CardDAV support sync-collection and filters.
  * Mail addresses, aliases and domains are stored in lowercase whoever
    writes them; a refused address names the rule it breaks; accounts get
    a free-text display name used by JMAP, SCIM and the send API.
  * Outbound delivery decides per recipient: one refused address no longer
    stops the others, no recipient is sent twice, and the remote reply is
    classified by its enhanced status code (mailbox full is retried, policy
    rejections are bounced but never suppress the address, a null MX fails
    at once). Retry timing is configurable and spread with jitter.
  * Inbound SMTP enforces the mailbox quota with 452 4.2.2, answers RCPT
    before MAIL with 503, and every error reply carries an enhanced code.
  * epistle status checks clamd from the mail container when the stack
    holds its socket.
  * The service account gets a subordinate id range no other account holds;
    an upgrade moves a range 0.10.1 shared with the first user.
  * Upgrades: stack update renders the compose file again, pins images by
    digest, dumps the database before restarting, checks health after it,
    and `epistle stack rollback` goes back to the previous binary;
    `epistle db upgrade` moves PostgreSQL to a new major; on SIGTERM the
    server drains open SMTP and IMAP sessions.
  * Stack: the server's PostgreSQL role is no longer a superuser, keys live
    outside data_dir, containers drop every capability they do not need,
    run read-only and have memory and process limits.
  * `epistle backup` works on servers that have sent mail (paths over 100
    bytes failed since 0.10.0).
  * The CLI marks states with symbols and colour on a terminal, shows
    progress on long commands, offers a list when an account, alias or
    domain is missing, and `epistle init` creates the first account with
    postmaster@ and abuse@.
  * PostgreSQL 18 is the minimum supported server.

